'''''Cyrtothyrea''''' is a small genus of [[Afrotropical realm|Afrotropical]] chafer beetles ([[Cetoniinae]]). They are about 9&nbsp;mm in size, black in colour with white dots or lines on the [[Thorax (insect anatomy)|thorax]], [[elytron|elytra]] and [[abdomen]]. Each species has its own distinctive pattern. ''Cyrtothyrea'' are active during the day, and are often found on flowers, while feeding on nectar. They are pollinators of several plant groups, including lily,<ref name=rod>{{cite journal|last1=Rodger|first1=James G.|last2=van Kleunen |first2=Mark |last3=Johnson |first3=Steven D. |title=Does Specialized Pollination Impede Plant Invasions? |journal=International Journal of Plant Sciences |date=May 2010 |volume=171 |issue=4 |pages=382–391 |doi=10.1086/651226 |url=http://kops.uni-konstanz.de/bitstream/handle/123456789/12497/Rodger_etal_2010_IJPS.pdf?sequence=1 |access-date=9 November 2014}}</ref> orchid,<ref name=peter>{{cite journal |last1=Peter |first1=C. I. |last2=Johnson |first2=S. D. |title=A pollinator shift explains floral divergence in an orchid species complex in South Africa |journal=Annals of Botany |date=9 October 2013 |volume=113 |issue=2 |pages=277–288 |doi=10.1093/aob/mct216 |pmid=24107684 |pmc=3890387}}</ref> protea<ref name=vega>{{cite journal |last1=de Vega |first1=C. |last2=Guzman |first2=B. |last3=Lachance |first3=M.-A. |last4=Steenhuisen |first4=S.-L. |last5=Johnson |first5=S. D. |last6=Herrera |first6=C. M. |title=Metschnikowia proteae sp. nov., a nectarivorous insect-associated yeast species from Africa |journal=International Journal of Systematic and Evolutionary Microbiology |date=9 March 2012 |volume=62 |issue=Pt 10 |pages=2538–2545 |doi=10.1099/ijs.0.040790-0 |pmid=22407789 |doi-access=free |hdl=10261/57423 |hdl-access=free }}</ref> and asclepiad species.<ref name=shut>{{cite journal |last1=Shuttleworth |first1=A. |last2=Johnson |first2=S.D. |title=Floral scents of chafer-pollinated asclepiads and a potential hybrid |journal=South African Journal of Botany |date=October 2010 |volume=76 |issue=4 |pages=770–778 |doi=10.1016/j.sajb.2010.07.023 |doi-access=free }}</ref> In the case of ''[[Lilium formosanum]]'', ''C. marginalis'' beetles feed on the [[Stamen|anthers]] or force themselves deep down into the [[perianth]] tubes to feed on nectar. At times they contact the [[Stigma (botany)|stigma]], which may also be used as a launch pad for taking off.<ref name=rod/>

==Species==
* ''[[Cyrtothyrea marginalis]]'' <small>(Swartz, 1817)</small>
* ''[[Cyrtothyrea rubriceps]]'' <small>(Raffray, 1877)</small>
* ''[[Cyrtothyrea testaceoguttata]]'' <small>(Blanchard, 1850)</small>

See also ''[[Cyrtothyrea rudebecki]]'' <small>Schein, 1960</small> possibly invalid as junior subjective synonym of ''[[Cyrtothyrea marginalis]]'' <small>(Swartz, 1817)</small>

